  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/D8E03F">Definition <span data-tag="D8E03F">5.3.1.7</span>.</a>filtered!$\infty $-category$\kappa $-filtered Let $\kappa $ be a regular cardinal, and let $\calC $ be a $\infty $-category. We will say that $\calC $ is <em>$\kappa $-filtered</em> if, for every $\kappa $-small simplicial set $K$ and every map $f: K \rightarrow \calC $, there exists a map $\overline{f}: K^{\triangleright } \rightarrow \calC $ extending $f$. (In other words, $\calC $ is $\kappa $-filtered if it has the extension property with respect to the inclusion $K \subseteq K^{\triangleright }$, for every $\kappa $-small simplicial set $K$.) </p>
  <p>We will say that $\calC $ is <em>filtered</em> if it is $\omega $-filtered. </p>
